  finding that all blood cells change is  very significant  and what we then saw is that these  characteristics revert back to normal in  most patients  once they've got over the disease and  made a full recovery  the test method from erlangen provides  doctors with yet another tool for better  understanding covid19  and its long-term effects so there is  some indication  of what could be causing long-covered  symptoms but a lot still needs to be  figured out let's try to unpack of what  is known  so far with me is matthew bartles  professor  of physical medicine and rehabilitation  at montefiore medical center in new york  welcome back to our program  matthew as we just saw in the report  abnormalities in the blood  could be a cause of long covet  could this finding help in the  prevention or even therapy of it  uh this is a very interesting finding  because  you know we have not really to this  point understood the exact  causes of long covid and it is a  systemic  type of disease and it manifests in many  ways as you had in your intro piece  the idea that something that's a blood  abnormality may contribute to a systemic  diseases  is not at all uh something that would be  unusual  many people are familiar with sickle  cell and although that's a genetic  condition and very different  having abnormalities of the blood cells  there cause many effects including  central neurological symptoms  of fatigue cause musculoskeletal  complaints  problems with the joints so having  something that is present all throughout  the body like the blood showing these  long-term abnormalities  may be a very important step in  understanding part of where  we may be getting the causes of our long  covet  10 to 20 percent of covet patients end  up suffering from  long covert symptoms like fatigue  shortness of breath headache  how long do those usually last  well that's a very good point we don't  really know we are  you know at this point only a year and a  half into the pandemic but 